### Describe the feature you'd like to request

I'm trying to abstract a logic in some of my routes. From my research I figured out that the regular middleware doesn't have a proper input support. Most of the previously asked related discussions are pointing towards the `experimental_standaloneMiddleware`. The idea is great but there seems to be a flaw in the implementation as of now. Please correct me if I'm wrong.

The type we provide for the generic `T` in `experimental_standaloneMiddleware<T>` has to match that of `.input()`'s. So if we have different routes with different inputs -that have a shared set of params for the middleware- it won't work.

### Describe the solution you'd like to see

What I had expected was to at least have a partial validation or merging of the two types for only the shared part of the inputs between the middleware and the procedure.

In other words: The `.input()` type must include the middleware params, but it can include other items as well.

### Describe alternate solutions

```ts
const schemaA = z.object({ valueA: z.string() });

const setQuizAnswerMiddleware = experimental_standaloneMiddleware(schemaA).create((opts) => {
  // The type-safe input
  const { input } = opts;

  // Create a new context value from the input...

  return opts.next({ ctx: { ... } });
});

const schemaB = z.object({ valueB: z.string() });

publicProcedure.input(schemaB).use(setQuizAnswerMiddleware)
            // Merged inputs
  .query(({ input: { valueA, valueB }, ctx: { ... } }): => ('Hello world');
```

### Additional information

There could also be an "isolated middleware" that takes it's own input, but doesn't expose them to the procedure. Its job is only to do some internal computation and add some values to the `ctx` object.

### 👨‍👧‍👦 Contributing

- [x] 🙋‍♂️ Yes, I'd be down to file a PR implementing this feature!

### Describe the feature you'd like to request

Simplify routers and procedure types in order to:

- reduce `.d.ts`-footprint (Context is duplicated everywhere)
- improve TS perf
- allow design to be more happy for defining standalone procedures without a router
- nicer procedures when hovering / autocompletion 


### Describe the solution you'd like to see


- built procedures should just be a simple structure containing `_input_in`, `_output_out` 
- instead of passing `TRouter extends AnyRouter` around everywhere, we should pass around `TConfig`
- (procedures are not aware of the `TConfig` once "built")
- when we create client helpers, we still need `TConfig`, so when we first initialize `TRPCClient` we  first extract `TConfig` and pass through the decorator

### Describe alternate solutions

not doing it

### Additional information

Currently a built router looks something like this:

```ts
export declare const router0: import("@trpc/server").CreateRouterInner<import("@trpc/server").RootConfig<{
    ctx: {};
    meta: object;
    errorShape: import("@trpc/server").DefaultErrorShape;
    transformer: import("@trpc/server").DefaultDataTransformer;
}>, {
    greeting: import("@trpc/server").BuildProcedure<"query", {
        _config: import("@trpc/server").RootConfig<{
            ctx: {};
            meta: object;
            errorShape: import("@trpc/server").DefaultErrorShape;
            transformer: import("@trpc/server").DefaultDataTransformer;
        }>;
        _meta: object;
        _ctx_out: {};
        _input_in: {
            who: string;
        };
        _input_out: {
            who: string;
        };
        _output_in: typeof import("@trpc/server").unsetMarker;
        _output_out: typeof import("@trpc/server").unsetMarker;
    }, string>;
}>;
```

Where after this, it'd look something like

```ts
export declare const router0: import("@trpc/server").CreateRouterInner<import("@trpc/server").RootConfig<{
    ctx: {};
    meta: object;
    errorShape: import("@trpc/server").DefaultErrorShape;
    transformer: import("@trpc/server").DefaultDataTransformer;
}>, {
    greeting: import("@trpc/server").Procedure<"query", {
        _input_in: {
            who: string;
        };
        _output_out: undefined;
    }, string>;
}>;
```

### 👨‍👧‍👦 Contributing

- [X] 🙋‍♂️ Yes, I'd be down to file a PR implementing this feature!

